# Prefer std::array over C-style arrays

Use `std::array<T, N>` for fixed-size buffers and member storage. Raw `T name[N]` decays to a pointer, hides the length from the type, and skips STL helpers (`size()`, range-for, `.at()` for debug bounds checks).

- **Member storage**: `std::array<ClosedQuarterSpan, kQuarterCount> closedQuarters_{};` not `ClosedQuarterSpan closedQuarters_[kQuarterCount]`.
- **Function parameters**: pass `const std::array<T, N>&` (or `std::span<const T>` when size is only known at runtime).
- **Stack locals** with a fixed count: `std::array<qint64, 3> components{};` not `qint64 components[3]`.
- Keep `QVector` / `std::vector` for dynamic length; `std::array` is for compile-time-fixed size only.
- `#include <array>` in headers that declare the member or parameter type.

```cpp
// BAD — size is not part of the type; array decays when passed around
ClosedQuarterSpan closedQuarters_[kQuarterCount] = {};
void labelAt(const ClosedQuarterSpan quarters[kQuarterCount]);

// GOOD — size is in the type; same indexing syntax
std::array<ClosedQuarterSpan, kQuarterCount> closedQuarters_{};
void labelAt(const std::array<ClosedQuarterSpan, kQuarterCount>& quarters);
```

When the index is already bounded by `kQuarterCount` loops, `operator[]` is fine. Prefer `.at(i)` when the index comes from external input and you want a hard bounds check in debug builds.
